the kernel's attic
==================

sys/attic/README

This is what used to be sys/UNUSED.

Fourteen files that are not built, are not included, and are not going
to be - and are kept anyway, because in most cases they are the only
copy of what they are.  This README is the fifteenth thing here and the
only one that is not one of those files.

It was sys/UNUSED, which was ignored (sys/.gitignore named it), so
nothing put there could be seen in git status and nothing put there
after 2020 was ever added.  The name it has now is the honest one, but
"attic" is ignored too, at any depth, by the root .gitignore - so the
root file re-includes this one directory by name.  That exception is
deliberate and commented there.  Do not add a rule to sys/.gitignore
that names this directory again.

what is in here, and where it came from
---------------------------------------

These are Micronix developers' working files, recovered off floppy
disks.  Two names are on them.  The MIO driver carries a header -

	/* Morrow Designs multiple Mult I/O driver */
	/* Len Edmondson */
	/* February, 1982 */

- and "L.W.E. March 1984" is on the inode-locking fix in the open.c
lineage below, which reads as the same person a second time, two years
later.  Morrow Designs is the vendor the S-100 machines in this tree
are built around; the changelog here is a Micronix developer's.

Which of them came off a floppy can be read off the file.  CP/M marks
end of file with ^Z and pads the last 128-byte record out with it, so a
file with a tail of ^Z, or with CRLF line endings, came through a CP/M
disk.  Four did:

	c		61 ^Z
	mio.bak		31 ^Z
	tty.bak		17 ^Z
	mset.bak	122 ^Z, 69 CR, and 140 NULs

The rest are plain ASCII with Unix line endings, so they were moved by
something that translated them on the way - a terminal, or a utility.

ps.c was one of those.  It is now cmd/ps/ps.c; see that directory.

the files
---------

changes		The developer's own changelog, about twenty-five entries,
		and the single most informative thing here.  It dates
		the rest: "Eliminated cpu member from proc structure /
		Eliminated cpu accounting as such" is why ps has its CPU
		column commented out; "Add argument value for proc 0 and
		proc 1 'swap' and 'init'.  Garbage appears under ps
		otherwise" is a change made for ps; and the 8-hertz
		clock, the IBM PC floppy support with DOS diskette
		auto-recognition, and the record locking below are all
		described here first.

		It also records the segalloc/segfree and ifill rewrites -
		"pick up where you left off search instead of brute
		force algorithm" - which is the shape the live
		sys/malloc.c still has.

omakefil	The kernel's original makefile, and the earliest one
		surviving.  It is a CP/M build through the Whitesmiths
		toolchain - AS = cpm a80, CC = cpm c80, LD = cpm lnk,
		ADDR = -tb0x1000 - and it links l1.o l2.o l3.o l4.o
		low.o io.o high.o through lnk -R.  The two makefiles in
		sys/ both describe the overlay link they replaced; this
		is what that link actually was.

		It has a line "# lock.o: lock.c inode.h proc.h sys.h
		lock.h" commented out, and it names sup.h, which is in
		no tree here.

		Not buildable, or useful to build: the compiler it calls
		has not existed for forty years.  It is here to be read
		when a question about the old link comes up.

omio.s		The A-Natural original of sys/mio.s.  The live file is a
		port - "Z80 translation of sys/mio.anat (asz dialect)" -
		so this is the source that port was made from, kept in
		the Whitesmiths assembler's dialect that nothing here
		runs.

mio.bak		The same driver, fourteen lines different.  An earlier
		or later revision of omio.s; the ^Z tail says it came
		off a disk.

multio.bak	An old sys/multio.c.  About 700 lines differ from the
		live one.

tty.bak		An old sys/tty.c.  About 1200 lines differ - much the
		longest divergence here.

mset.bak	The ONE surviving mset.c; sys/mset.c is gone from the
		tree.  "Set the baud rate on serial ports."  Its first
		128 bytes are not source at all - they are a stray CP/M
		record of binary garbage sitting in front of the real
		file, which is intact behind them and CRLF throughout.

getc.s,		A-Natural sources for _getc, with the C they were
ogetc.s		written from preserved in the driver's own comments.
		The two differ by whitespace on one line.  No live
		counterpart.

		The "/" at the start of a line in these files is
		A-Natural's comment marker, not corruption - the tree's
		own intrpt.s uses the same scaffolding to quote the
		original declarations.

open.bak,	Two versions of open.c - really one version twice, see
oopen.c		below.

pc.c		A developer's process watcher: opens /dev/mem, reads the
		table pointer at 0x1003, then loops seeking each
		process's u page and printing u.pc.  It is the same
		kernel hook ps uses, and it is the only other thing
		here that reads the running kernel rather than being
		part of it.

README.filesystem
		A design note on the filesystem's superblock: 100 free
		inodes held there, repopulation by scanning the inode
		blocks, allocation from the end, and a block free list
		whose entry 0 is the indirect head.  It cites the v6
		manual and names ialloc.c and balloc.c, both of which
		are still in the tree and still work that way.

ps.c		Moved to cmd/ps/ps.c and ported there.  It compiles
		and links, it is in cmd/GNUmakefile's DIRS, and ps.c's
		own header comment is the record of what the port cost.
		This is the only ps.c there ever was - the ps binary
		installed in filesystem/bin is an older, different
		build, not another copy of this.

c		Not a source file.  One 128-byte CP/M record - a
		fragment of C:

			if (!tty->mstate & DILcount && ...

		- padded out with 61 ^Z.  It is one record off a disk,
		kept only because it is not identifiable as anything
		else.

the three open.c versions
-------------------------

sys/open.c is the live one.  open.bak and oopen.c here are the history
behind it, and they answer differently than they look:

  * open.bak and oopen.c are THE SAME CODE.  The function set is
    identical - open, pipe, iopen, close, fclose, iclose, falloc,
    oalloc, ofile, imode - and a line-by-line diff of the two is
    entirely formatting.  open.bak is Whitesmiths house style:
    tab-indented bodies, the opening brace alone on its own indented
    line, seven-space comment indents, "irelse (ip)" with a space
    before the paren.  oopen.c is that same code reformatted to
    4-space K&R.  oopen.c is 625 bytes larger only because spaces
    replaced tabs.  They are v6 C throughout - fast, NULL, ip->mode,
    ITYPE, IDIR, ICIO, IBIO - and include "sys.h" and friends by bare
    name, out of the flat /usr/include.

  * Both carry the same fix, in iopen()'s ICIO case:

	/*
	 * Don't sleep with a locked inode.
	 * L.W.E. March 1984
	 */
	ip->count++;
	irelse(ip);
	copen(dev, writ);	/* sets u.p->tty */
	ilock(ip);
	ip->count--;

    iopen() takes the inode lock, so it must drop it across copen() or
    the process sleeps holding a lock.  The date says the bug was found
    and this is the repair.

  * The live open.c is that same file ported: v7 inode names (i_mode,
    i_addr[], IFMT, IFDIR, IFCHR, IFBLK), ccc dialect (register, 0 for
    NULL), and <types.h> / <sys/...> / <errno.h> includes.  It keeps
    the March 1984 note, cleanly indented.

    It also carries ONE behavioural change that is written down
    nowhere.  iclose()'s IFCHR case gained the same discipline iopen
    applies to copen:

	ip->count++;
	irelse(ip);
	cclose(dev, writ);
	ilock(ip);
	ip->count--;

    - the count/irelse/ilock/count dance, with no comment at all.
    The tell is the formatting: the block above it still has
    open.bak's seven-space "       /*" and "\t *" continuation, while
    every other comment in the live file is indented normally.  So the
    live file descends from the open.bak lineage, and the close-side
    lock protection was added later by someone who did not write it
    down.  Whoever changes iclose() should know it is there and why.

two things recorded here that are not in the tree any more
----------------------------------------------------------

Record locking.  The changelog describes it in full: two system calls,
numbers 49 and 50, "lock (fd, count)" and "unlock (fd)", locking the
region from the current seek position for count bytes, largest 64K-1,
returning EBUSY if the region is locked and ENFILE if the lock table is
full, with close() releasing a file's locks and no sleeps in either
call.  omakefil has a lock.o rule commented out.  include/sys/lock.h is
still in the tree with the table definitions.  lock.c is gone.

The cpu member of struct proc.  Removed, with cpu accounting, per the
changelog - and include/sys/proc.h still has the "UCHAR cpu" it used to
be, inside an #ifdef notdef, with a comment asking "why ?".  ps.c has
its CPU column commented out in three places to match, and the
recovered man page, man/man1/oldps.1, still documents a CPU column that
no longer exists.  (It is oldps.1 and not ps.1 because this tree's ps
has a page of its own beside its source, cmd/ps/ps.1; the rename is the
same one the 1982 sh page got, to oldsh.1.)

Both were real features with real code.  Neither has a source file
here.  That is the argument for this directory existing: these files
are what is left of the work, and the tree is where the parts that
survived it live.

what was thrown away
--------------------

Seven files came with a compiler's scratch: oopen.nam, oopen.tok,
oopen.x, pc.nam, pc.tok, pc.x and ps.x, from a build someone ran in the
directory in September.  A .nam, a .tok and a .x are what ccc leaves
beside a source, and sys/.gitignore already ignores them by extension.
They were deleted rather than kept: they are regenerable from the .c
beside them, and they are not history.
